# Instructions

See AGENTS.md for the full repository conventions. This file holds working
notes: reusable lessons first, then a scratchpad for the active task.

# Lessons

## User Specified Lessons

- You have a python venv in ./venv. Always use (activate) it when doing python
  development. First, to check whether 'uv' is available, use `which uv`. If
  that's the case, first activate the venv, and then use `uv pip install` to
  install packages. Otherwise, fall back to `pip`.
- Due to Cursor's limit, when you use `git` and `gh` and need to submit a
  multiline commit message, first write the message in a file, and then use
  `git commit -F <filename>` or similar command to commit. And then remove the
  file. Include "[Cursor] " in the commit message and PR title.

## Cursor learned

- This environment has no `uv`; plain `venv/bin/pip` works. CPU torch comes
  from `pip install --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu torch`.
- `python3 -m venv` fails on this image until `apt-get update && apt-get
  install -y python3.12-venv` (ensurepip is stripped).
- Sources live in `src/` but the import name is `minimodel`
  (`package-dir = {minimodel = "src"}` + an explicit `packages` list in
  pyproject; `packages.find` mis-maps subpackages with a renamed root).
- PyYAML follows YAML 1.1: `3e-4` parses as a *string* (needs `3.0e-4`).
  `core.config.parse_scalar` therefore float()s bare scientific notation so
  `--set training.lr=3e-4` works; keep decimals in YAML files themselves.
- Python `re` has no `\p{L}`; letters are `[^\W\d_]` and "symbol runs" need a
  negative-lookahead class. Any pre-tokenizer split pattern must be *total*
  (`|.` fallback + a test), otherwise unmatched chars are silently dropped
  from the corpus.
- `torch.load(weights_only=True)` for model weights always; trainer state
  (RNG/optimizer objects) cannot use it - keep the two in separate files.
- Muon's Newton-Schulz coefficients do NOT converge singular values to 1;
  they compress the spectrum into ~[0.2, 2]. Test the ratio, not orthogonality.
  Route embeddings/lm_head/1-D params to AdamW, never Muon.
- Newly initialised byte-BPE models can emit isolated invalid UTF-8 bytes:
  streaming decoders must cap their "wait for valid UTF-8" buffer (we use 4
  tokens) or they stall/accumulate U+FFFD forever.
- Weight-shared/looped blocks need a KV cache keyed by *call order*
  (`begin_forward()` rewinds a cursor), not by module identity; hybrid
  recurrent state rides in the same cache (`cache.recurrent_states`) so
  incremental decoding exactly matches full forward (tested to 1e-4).
- `nn.Module.apply(normal_init)` tramples special inits - re-assert zeros/
  ones/constants (LoRA-up, gates, timestep scales, RMSNorm weights, RG-LRU
  log-lambda) *after* the generic pass, in that order.
- DPO/GRPO logging: call `.detach()` before `float(tensor)` on anything that
  requires grad, or PyTorch warns on every step.
- argparse subparser help: reaching `parser._subparsers._actions` is the only
  way to print a *group's* help when no subcommand was given; wrap it once in
  `cli.main` and reuse.
- matplotlib in CI/headless: `matplotlib.use("Agg")` before pyplot import,
  and provide ASCII fallbacks so nothing hard-requires it.
- Generated-artifact pattern that works: generator script + committed output
  + `--check` mode wired into tests/CI (used for both template families).

# Scratchpad

## Task 2: SLM research to beat Glint-2 (2026-08-02)

Status: head-to-head WIN achieved + pipeline hardened. Key artifacts in research/.
[X] Identified Glint-2 (HF Glint-Research/Glint-2). Reverse-engineered: released
    checkpoint is 1.71M (loop+coda), NOT advertised 1.06M pure-loop; their
    generate.py can't load their own weights (strict). Faithful loader in
    research/baselines/glint2_model.py.
[X] Model-agnostic eval harness (BLiMP/ARC/byte-ppl). Validated: ARC 36.78 vs
    36.80; their "wikitext ppl" == byte-normalised (2^BPB) not token ppl.
    BLiMP only comparable within one harness (we get 66.4).
[X] Arch bake-off @1.7M/2.5M tokens: dense wins per-token (byte-ppl 16.3) vs
    looped 18-19 — matches iso-depth 2026 literature.
[X] Loop robustness: Glint-2 spread 35x (3.5@8 -> 125@16, gibberish); ours with
    stabilizers+Poisson span 1.04-1.19x and improve to loops=16. Controlled
    3-variant experiment isolates cause = our value-residual/timestep/outer-gate.
[X] Optimizer: Muon >> AdamW >> Lion on looped (val 3.12 vs 4.54 vs 5.38).
[X] Contender (dense, 49M TinyStories tokens, 32min/4CPU): byte-ppl 1.525
    in-dist vs Glint-2's 2.405 on same TinyStories dist = 37% WIN. WikiText gap
    (16.3 vs 3.18) is data coverage (fix: add fineweb-edu), not train quality.
[X] Pipeline shipped+tested: effort ladder (inference/search.py), quality probe
    (inference/quality_probe.py), flexible looped arch (prelude/coda/tied/
    stabilizers), log-normal Poisson loop sampling, truncated backprop, byte-ppl.
[X] Reports 00-04, 07-09 written; 05 (ffn) running. 472 tests pass, lint clean.
[ ] finish ffn ablation -> report 05; final commit + push (PR #1 updated).

Lessons (task 2):
- CPU (4 core) can't match Glint-2's training scale; win on matched-distribution
  perplexity + robustness + features, project absolute-quality recipe to GPU.
- 22x FFN (Glint-2) is very slow on CPU (~4.6K tok/s); dense ~32K, ffn768 ~12K.
- byte-per-perplexity (2^BPB) is the fair tokenizer-independent metric; a 4096
  BPE gives ~4 bytes/token so token-ppl and byte-ppl differ ~4x.
- gotcha: root .gitignore `data/` also matches research/data/ -> had to
  re-include research/data/results/ explicitly to track result JSONs.

